\subsection{Extended LLM-as-Judge Evaluation}
\label{sec:llm_extended}

Table~\ref{tab:llm_extended} extends the zero-shot LLM-as-judge baseline (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}) with few-shot prompting and an alternative model, all evaluated on the SWE-Agent conf${\geq}$2 test set ($N{=}1{,}824$).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Setting} & \textbf{Macro-F1} & \textbf{Parse Fail} & \textbf{Cost} \\
\midrule
Claude Sonnet 4.6 & zero-shot & 0.6625 & 0\% & \$11.66 \\
Claude 3.5 Sonnet & 5-shot & 0.751 & 0\% & \$36.08 \\
GPT-5.5 & zero-shot & 0.746 & 22.4\% & \$11.62 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Extended LLM-as-judge evaluation on SWE-Agent conf${\geq}$2 ($N{=}1{,}824$). Few-shot prompting improves Claude by 8.9\,pp but triples cost. GPT-5.5 achieves comparable performance but with a 22.4\% parse failure rate (unparseable responses default to majority class). Even the best LLM configuration (0.751, in-domain) trails structural cross-coding-agent transfer (GB 0.967 on OpenHands) by 21.6\,pp, despite the LLM's in-domain advantage.}
\label{tab:llm_extended}
\end{table}

